# Shopify Platinum Refactor Rules

1. Shopify architecture
   1. Use Shopify 2.0 sections, blocks, snippets.
   2. Keep section file as orchestrator: assignments, schema, high level layout only.
   3. UI chunks go to snippets with explicit props.

2. Liquid rules
   1. Use render, never include.
   2. Pass every dependency explicitly to snippets.
   3. Compute variables at top of file.
   4. Any snippet must begin with a Props comment block listing required and optional props and defaults.

3. Product availability rule
   1. The section must not assume product exists.
   2. Section schema must include a product setting.
   3. Orchestrator chooses mc_product using product when present, otherwise section.settings.product.
   4. If mc_product is blank, render a clear placeholder message visible only in theme editor.

4. CSS rules
   1. No global CSS. All selectors must be scoped under a unique root selector per section instance.
   2. Root selector must be an id on the section wrapper: #MagicCover__{{ section.id }}.
   3. Use CSS variables for any values coming from section.settings.
   4. Only allow a small inline style block in the section file that assigns CSS variables on #MagicCover__{{ section.id }}.
   5. All other CSS lives in assets/magic-cover.css.

5. JavaScript rules
   1. No jQuery.
   2. Use a Custom Element for section logic. Tag name: magic-cover.
   3. Web component must be multi instance safe and store all state on the element instance.
   4. No module global state. No singleton caches.
   5. Query scope: use this.querySelector and this.querySelectorAll for internal nodes.
      If you must touch window or document, you must add listeners in connectedCallback and remove in disconnectedCallback.
   6. Custom element registration must be guarded:
      Only define if customElements.get('magic-cover') is falsy.
   7. Configuration must be read from data attributes on the host element.
      If JSON is used, it must be serialized with json and escaped for attribute context in Liquid.

6. IDs and accessibility
   1. Any id in snippets must be unique per instance using a passed id_prefix, typically section.id.
   2. Labels must point to inputs using for and id.
   3. Errors must be accessible, use aria describedby and aria live when appropriate.
   4. Focus states must be visible.

7. Performance
   1. Progressive enhancement: core UX must work with HTML and CSS first.
   2. JS must be deferred.
   3. Lazy load images below the fold only.
   4. Preview area must reserve space using aspect ratio to prevent layout shift.

8. Theme editor behavior
   1. Do not assume full page reloads.
   2. All cleanup must happen in disconnectedCallback.
   3. If observers, timers, media streams are used, they must be disposed.

